  "evidence": "Compared the finite-field detecting statement with its number-field valuation proof, the two compactness arguments and the telescoping lemma for wild automorphisms. Independently enumerated the order-20 example.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "TIC-01",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "One inertia element generates all the finite tame images at a chosen place",
      "anchor": "inputs-tame-inertia-lift",
      "statement": "For a prolongation of a finite place of residue characteristic p on the algebraic closure Ω of a number field, there is γ∈I such that the powers of γ are dense in I/P, where P is wild inertia. The same γ may be used for every continuous additive ℓ-primary character, for every ℓ≠p.",
      "hypotheses": "Fix a prolongation and use the Krull topology. The archive constructs γ at a valuation of an algebraic closure of a number field. Its proof uses algebraicity over ℚ to control the value of every nonzero element through a power of p.",
      "definitions": [
        "P consists of automorphisms σ with |σ(y)/y−1|<1 for every y≠0.",
        "If r_n^(n!)=p, the finite cyclic tame image is obtained by mapping σ to σ(r_n)/r_n modulo the principal units."
      ],
